<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Find meanings, look up names and define a reusable concept set.
</div>

This chapter uses the public synthetic GiBleed practice connection from [Getting started](book-00-start.html).
**You need:** the packages and the matching practice connection described in
[Before you start](book-00-start.html#before-you-start).
Run the sections in order in one R session. All printed results below were computed
with dsOMOP and dsOMOPClient 2.10.5. They are teaching data, not clinical findings.

## Start this chapter

Start a fresh R session in the `book-work` directory created in
[Getting started](book-00-start.html#get-the-book-helpers). Run this setup first:

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
source("tools/book/practice.R")
practice <- if (book_driver == "DSLite") book_practice("gibleed") else book_connect("gibleed")
conns <- practice$conns
names(conns)
```

The `site` connection is ready. The shared `connection.R` selects DSLite or Opal and resolves the resource names.

## Search a name, then narrow the meaning

A concept is a public vocabulary meaning. Start broadly, then constrain the
domain (kind of clinical fact), vocabulary (coding system) and standard status.
Standard concepts are the preferred OMOP targets; classification concepts group
them, and source concepts may map to them. None of this establishes patient use.

```{r search}
hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Asthma", conns = conns)
knitr::kable(head(hits$pooled[c("concept_name", "domain_id", "vocabulary_id", "standard_concept")], 8))
```

Now keep valid standard SNOMED conditions. The explicit filters make the search
reproducible; a word alone can also match findings or history-of conditions.

```{r filtered-search}
conditions <- ds.omop.concept.search("Asthma", domain = "Condition",
  vocabulary = "SNOMED", standard_only = TRUE, valid = TRUE, conns = conns)
knitr::kable(head(conditions$pooled[c("concept_name", "concept_class_id")], 8))
asthma_id <- conditions$pooled$concept_id[conditions$pooled$concept_name == "Asthma"]
stopifnot(length(asthma_id) == 1L)
```

**Exercise 1 (basic).** Broaden the query to non-standard concepts. Does that
change the patient population yet?

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r broader-search}
broad <- ds.omop.concept.search("Asthma", standard_only = FALSE, conns = conns)
knitr::kable(head(broad$pooled[c("concept_name", "vocabulary_id", "standard_concept")], 8))
```

No: this is a catalogue search. No clinical rows have been selected.

</details>

## Read a concept and its mappings

`lookup()` returns the compact identity fields; the filtered search above
also returns the full metadata used here. The concept class says what sort of vocabulary item this is. Valid dates delimit
its vocabulary validity, not the dates of patient records. An invalid reason is
about a vocabulary entry, not a patient's diagnosis.

```{r inspect}
asthma_info <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(asthma_id, conns = conns)
knitr::kable(conditions$pooled[conditions$pooled$concept_id == asthma_id, c("concept_name", "standard_concept",
  "concept_class_id", "valid_start_date", "valid_end_date", "invalid_reason")])
```

A source code's **Maps to** relationship can identify its standard target.
Inspect the relationship direction and target before using mapped expansion.
A standard concept may map to itself; that is not an additional diagnosis.

```{r maps-to}
mappings <- ds.omop.concept.relationships(asthma_id,
  relationship_id = "Maps to", conns = conns)
print(data.frame(returned_relationships = NROW(mappings$pooled)))
```

An empty relationship result means this practice vocabulary has no returned
mapping for the requested direction. It does not establish that all source codes
are standard; inspect the source code itself in your own vocabulary.

## Walk down and up the hierarchy

Descendants are more specific meanings. Ancestors are broader meanings. A
SNOMED parent can have many clinically different children: inspect before pooling.

```{r descendants}
desc <- ds.omop.concept.descendants(asthma_id, include_self = FALSE, conns = conns)
knitr::kable(head(desc$pooled["concept_name"], 8))
```

```{r ancestors}
parents <- ds.omop.concept.ancestors(asthma_id, conns = conns)
knitr::kable(head(parents$pooled["concept_name"], 8))
```

For medicines, ATC is a classification vocabulary, so the default
`standard_only = TRUE` can hide the class you need. This is a common pitfall.

```{r atc}
atc <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, vocabulary = "ATC",
  standard_only = FALSE, conns = conns)
print(data.frame(returned_ATC_classes = NROW(atc$pooled)))
```

If this subset vocabulary returns zero ATC classes, stop: no ATC-class study
can be inferred from ingredient names. An authorised resource with that vocabulary
is needed. Do not fabricate a class or silently substitute an ingredient.

Only use a returned class when its description matches the protocol. The drug
utilisation case study below expands the selected class and inspects its members.

## Check that the concept occurs here

Vocabulary presence is different from clinical prevalence. Prevalence is a
protected aggregate over a **table**, with `metric = "persons"` by default.
It has no `concept_ids` filter: select the row from the returned protected table.
A concept outside the requested top list need not be absent.

```{r prevalence}
prevalence <- ds.omop.concept.prevalence("condition_occurrence",
  metric = "persons", top_n = 20, scope = "pooled", conns = conns)
knitr::kable(prevalence$pooled)
```

Locate instead accepts concept IDs and reports which clinical columns use them.
It is also protected: it is not a way to recover exact small cell counts.

```{r locate}
locations <- ds.omop.concept.locate(asthma_id, scope = "pooled", conns = conns)
knitr::kable(locations$pooled)
```

Read a band as a band, a suppressed result as unavailable, and an absent top-list
entry as inconclusive. Never replace those with zero in a study denominator.

## Save the definition, including exclusions

Save the local definition rather than copying a transient list of search rows.
This preserves whether descendants and mapped concepts belong in the set.

```{r concept-set}
asthma <- omop_concept_set(asthma_id, descendants = TRUE)
asthma_exact <- omop_concept_set(asthma_id, descendants = FALSE)
class(asthma)
```

**Exercise 2 (intermediate).** Exclude the seed itself while keeping its
children. Save the definition and read it back without accessing patients.

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r excluded-set}
children_only <- omop_concept_set(asthma_id, descendants = TRUE,
  exclude = omop_concept_set(asthma_id, descendants = FALSE))
concept_file <- "book-concepts.rds"
saveRDS(list(asthma = asthma, children_only = children_only), concept_file)
saved_sets <- readRDS(concept_file)
identical(saved_sets$children_only, children_only)
```

The result is TRUE. An excluded set can itself use descendants when you intend
to remove an entire subtree. Keep these definitions with the study protocol.

</details>

## Find inpatient and outpatient visits

The setting is `visit_concept_id`; `visit_type_concept_id` describes provenance.
The names below produce the IDs used in the visit chapters.

```{r visits}
visit_hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Visit", domain = "Visit", conns = conns)
visit_catalogue <- visit_hits$pooled[visit_hits$pooled$concept_name %in%
  c("Inpatient Visit", "Outpatient Visit"), ]
knitr::kable(visit_catalogue[c("concept_name", "standard_concept")])
visit_ids <- setNames(visit_catalogue$concept_id, visit_catalogue$concept_name)
visit_sets <- lapply(visit_ids, omop_concept_set)
ds.omop.concept.locate(unname(visit_ids), scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
```

## Find the questionnaire questions, answers and causes

The questionnaire fixture is explicitly synthetic and separate from GiBleed.
Its local concepts are teaching examples, not codes to copy into a clinical study.
Search the whole small catalogue so the question, answer and cause roles are
visible together. Later questionnaire chapters refer back to this catalogue.

```{r questionnaire-catalogue}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(conns)
source("tools/book/fixture.R")
practice <- book_fixture()
conns <- practice$conns
questionnaire <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, standard_only = FALSE,
  limit = 100, conns = conns)$pooled
knitr::kable(questionnaire[c("concept_name", "domain_id", "standard_concept")])
question_ids <- setNames(questionnaire$concept_id, questionnaire$concept_name)
education_set <- omop_concept_set(unname(question_ids["Education question"]))
cause_sets <- lapply(question_ids[c("Myocardial infarction", "Pneumonia")], omop_concept_set)
```

The education question selects observations. Secondary and University education
are possible answers in `value_as_concept_id`. Myocardial infarction and Pneumonia
are coded causes in death. Local fixture concepts have no external mapping or
hierarchy; do not invent SNOMED or ATC ancestry for them.

```{r questionnaire-presence}
question_presence <- ds.omop.concept.prevalence("observation", scope = "pooled", conns = conns)
knitr::kable(question_presence$pooled[setdiff(names(question_presence$pooled), "concept_id")])
```

```{r cause-presence}
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.locate(unname(question_ids[c("Myocardial infarction", "Pneumonia")]),
  scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled)
```

## Find haemoglobin, glucose and their units in Synthea

Switch to the numeric resource. The vocabulary spells haemoglobin “Hemoglobin”.
Select the precise test and specimen, not every hit containing the word.

```{r numeric-catalogue}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(conns)
unlink(practice$path)
practice <- book_connect("numeric")
conns <- practice$conns
lab_hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Hemoglobin", domain = "Measurement", conns = conns)$pooled
knitr::kable(lab_hits[c("concept_name", "vocabulary_id", "standard_concept")])
hb_id <- lab_hits$concept_id[lab_hits$concept_name == "Hemoglobin [Mass/volume] in Blood"]
glucose_hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Glucose", domain = "Measurement", conns = conns)$pooled
knitr::kable(glucose_hits[c("concept_name", "vocabulary_id", "standard_concept")])
lab_sets <- list(haemoglobin = omop_concept_set(hb_id),
  glucose = omop_concept_set(glucose_hits$concept_id))
```

These glucose hits are a catalogue, not yet an interchangeable measurement set.
The chronic-disease study selects a single specimen and concentration measure.

```{r lab-units}
units <- ds.omop.value.counts("measurement", "unit_concept_id", concept_id = hb_id,
  scope = "pooled", conns = conns)
knitr::kable(units$pooled)
```

Counts describe recorded unit use under disclosure control. A vocabulary lookup
of “milligram per deciliter” alone cannot establish that the lab was stored that way.

**Exercise 3 (advanced).** Check glucose unit use and save its catalogue together
with the haemoglobin set. Why is choosing all glucose hits premature?

<details><summary>Solution 3</summary>

```{r glucose-units}
glucose_units <- ds.omop.value.counts("measurement", "unit_concept_id",
  concept_id = glucose_hits$concept_id[glucose_hits$concept_name == "Glucose [Mass/volume] in Serum or Plasma"], scope = "pooled", conns = conns)
knitr::kable(glucose_units$pooled)
saveRDS(list(sets = lab_sets, glucose_catalogue = glucose_hits,
  visit_sets = visit_sets, education = education_set, causes = cause_sets,
  questionnaire_catalogue = questionnaire), concept_file)
identical(readRDS(concept_file)$sets, lab_sets)
```

Keep `book-concepts.rds` with your study files. In a later session,
`readRDS("book-concepts.rds")$education` reuses the saved education definition;
the questionnaire catalogue records the IDs used in subsequent examples.

Different specimens, mass versus molar concentrations and urine versus blood
measurements need different definitions. Preserve that decision with the set.

</details>

## Recap

Search, inspect standard status and mappings, explore ancestry, check protected
clinical use, then save the definition. The subsequent chapters reuse these
catalogues; the case studies repeat the same process for their research question.

**Next:** [5. A first recipe](book-05-recipe.html).
